# The Origin of Starlight

In the beginning, the world was shrouded in endless twilight. The Sun ruled the day with fierce brightness, while the Moon governed the night in cold darkness. Between them stretched a gray, forgotten time when neither would shine.

A young weaver named Lyra grew tired of this dim world. She gathered threads of silver from the Moon's reflection in still waters and golden strands from the Sun's rays at dawn. For seven years, she wove these threads together, creating a magnificent tapestry that shimmered with impossible light.

When she completed her work, Lyra climbed the highest mountain and held the tapestry toward the sky. The Sun and Moon, curious about this creation that held both their essences, drew closer to examine it. As they did, Lyra tore the tapestry into countless pieces and scattered them across the heavens.

Each fragment became a star, carrying both warmth and gentle radiance. The Sun and Moon were so moved by Lyra's gift that they agreed to share the sky more gracefully, creating the golden hours of dusk and dawn.

And so, humans received the stars—eternal reminders that even the mightiest powers can be transformed through patience, creativity, and the courage to bridge what seems forever divided.
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

